Chapter 2
Literature Review

The dissertation sits at the intersection of four research streams, none of which has previously been brought to bear on the comprehensive measurement of knowledge-system loss from catastrophic events. This chapter surveys each stream and identifies the specific gaps the dissertation addresses.

2.1 Economic Historiography of WWII
The dominant tradition measures war costs through military and civilian casualties, fiscal expenditure, and physical capital destruction (Harrison 1998; Overy 1994). Human capital losses, when considered, are typically valued using the replacement cost of education (Broadberry & Harrison 2005). Such approaches treat the dead as a one-time stock depletion; they do not model the flow of future output that is extinguished. Recent work by Waldinger (2010, 2012) and Moser, Voena & Waldinger (2014) demonstrates that the dismissal of Jewish academics in Nazi Germany caused long-term declines in the productivity of the students they would have trained, providing a micro-foundation for the intergenerational transmission of knowledge loss. Crucially, Waldinger’s estimated elasticity—a reduction of approximately 1.8 lifetime publications per affected doctoral student—offers an empirically calibrated parameter that this dissertation can directly embed in its mentorship multiplier. No existing study, however, extends this insight to the global mortality shock of WWII, nor integrates it with network and institutional disruption at scale.

2.2 Endogenous Growth Theory and Human Capital
Endogenous growth models (Romer 1990; Jones 1995) formalise the link between the stock of human capital and the rate of innovation. They predict that a permanent shock that reduces the number of knowledge producers can permanently lower the level of output and, under some parameterisations, the growth rate. This dissertation adopts a Romer-Jones expanding-varieties structure as a fallback counterfactual model when synthetic control methods fail. The aggregate human capital analysis uses Mincerian returns to education (Mincer 1974) applied to excess deaths to estimate the economy-wide loss of productive capacity. The dissertation advances this literature by using granular, individual-level data on knowledge producers rather than national aggregates, and by separating the elite innovative stratum from the general labour force in order to examine the distributional character of the shock.

2.3 Network Science of Innovation
Knowledge creation is a networked enterprise. Collaboration networks channel ideas, enable specialisation, and create spillovers (Burt 2004; Wuchty, Jones & Uzzi 2007). The expulsion of Jewish scientists fragmented European scholarly networks, but no study has systematically measured the structural disruption of formal and informal ties across multiple fields. This dissertation reconstructs co-authorship and professional society networks for physics and mathematics in the 1930s and 1940s, using the descriptive toolkit of graph theory to produce a Network Disruption Index. Critically, it follows the literature in recognising that the relationship between network topology and innovative output is context-dependent and not reducible to a single elasticity. Therefore, the NDI is presented as a descriptive profile rather than a causal input to the FIM; the gap between micro-level FHCO and macro-level FIM becomes itself an object of analysis, offering insight into the relative importance of individual versus systemic loss.

2.4 Counterfactual Methods and the Philosophy of History
The fundamental problem of causal inference—the missing counterfactual—is especially acute when the event of interest is unique and global (Lewis 1973; Ferguson 1997). Fogel’s (1964) Railroads and American Economic Growth set the standard for transparent counterfactual speculation, a standard this dissertation emulates by stating all assumptions, reporting uncertainty ranges, and inviting readers to vary parameters. The synthetic control method (Abadie, Diamond & Hainmueller 2010) provides the most defensible statistical approach to constructing a no-war trajectory, although its applicability is limited by the availability of unexposed donor countries. For cases where donor pools are insufficient—most acutely the USSR—the dissertation supplements the synthetic control with a calibrated structural growth model and a pre-war growth-accounting reality check, ensuring that the counterfactual retrodicts observed pre-1939 patterns before being projected forward.

2.5 Catastrophic Risk and Lost Futures
The literature on global catastrophic and existential risk (Bostrom 2002, 2013; Ord 2020) emphasises the importance of preserving long-term potential, but it has lacked a formal, calibrated framework for measuring the cost of historical catastrophes. This dissertation provides a prototype for such a framework, tested against the largest conflict in human history and validated through a placebo study on the 1918 influenza pandemic. By demonstrating that bounded, transparent quantification is possible even for the most profound human catastrophe, it opens a pathway for applying similar architectures to other past and future risks, from pandemics to nuclear war to climate-induced scientific fragmentation.

