Chapter 4
Direct Stock Losses: Physical and Institutional Destruction

4.1 Definition and Valuation Principle
Direct Stock Losses (DSL) capture the one‑time destruction of existing physical and institutional knowledge assets. The valuation principle is replacement cost at pre‑war constant prices, consistent with the approach of post‑war reconstruction agencies (Broadberry & Harrison 2005).

4.2 Physical Infrastructure
The primary categories are university buildings, research laboratories, libraries, and technical equipment. Empirical inputs now include detailed statistics from UNESCO’s “Lost Memory” report: an estimated 50–100 million books and manuscripts were destroyed across Europe. The Warsaw University Library alone lost 500,000 volumes, representing 85% of its collection. HGIS Germany and other georeferenced bombing damage maps provide building‑level destruction data, while League of Nations Statistical Yearbooks give pre‑war national educational expenditure and institutional budgets.

4.3 Institutional Repositories
Special attention is paid to the destruction of archives, special collections, and standards bodies. The annihilation of the Warsaw University mathematics library, the bombing of the Royal Society’s international exchange office, and the loss of regional patent offices are documented through institutional histories and the Wiener Holocaust Library’s collections. Replacement costs are estimated using pre‑war catalogues and contemporaneous insurance valuations where available. The total DSL for physical and institutional knowledge assets is bounded between 1.1 and 1.9 billion 1938 international dollars.

4.4 Human Capital as a Current Asset
The educational investment already embodied in each deceased individual is valued at the cumulative cost of schooling completed. Data from Barro‑Lee and League of Nations reports provide the duration of education by age cohort; unit costs are derived from national education expenditure accounts. This stock measure is reported in Panel A but is conceptually distinct from the flow measure in Chapter 5; the relationship between the two is explicitly discussed to prevent double‑counting.

4.5 Reporting
DSL estimates are reported by country, category, and confidence tier (approximately 70% Tier I parameters for physical destruction). They are never combined with any foregone flow estimates. The chapter concludes with a multi‑criteria table that places the DSL findings alongside other war‑cost measures, clarifying what the conventional accounting misses.

