Appendix C: Derivation of the FHCO Model and Mentorship Multiplier

This appendix lays out the mathematical derivation of the core Foregone Human Capital Output (FHCO) model. All equations are theoretical constructs; no empirical estimation has been performed.

C.1 Basic FHCO Equation

For an individual i who died prematurely at age a_d:

\text{FHCO}_i = \sum_{t=a_d}^{T_{\max}} S_i(t) \cdot P_i(t) \cdot O_i(t) \cdot e^{-\rho(t-a_d)} \cdot M_i(a_d)

where:

· S_i(t) is the counterfactual survival probability from period life tables (no war).
· P_i(t) is the age‑specific productivity factor (0–1), normalised to peak productivity.
· O_i(t) is the expected annual output at peak productivity, derived from the individual’s pre‑war record or field averages.
· \rho is the discount rate (sensitivity: 0%, 2%, 5%).
· T_{\max} is set at 75 years.
· M_i(a_d) is the mentorship multiplier defined below.

C.2 Age‑Productivity Profiles P_i(t) – Planned Calibration

· Mathematics and physics: A loess curve would be fitted to all unique author‑year observations in zbMATH/JFM (223,276 digitised documents) and the WoS backfile, normalised so that the maximum observed publication rate equals 1.
· Engineering and chemistry: An analogous curve would be derived from age‑patenting frequency in PATSTAT and NBER Patent Data, where peak inventorship occurs at 35–45 years.
· Major recognitions: The age distribution of Nobel Prizes and academy elections (1901‑1938) would be used to fit a Poisson process model for the probability of a high‑value breakthrough at each age.

C.3 Mentorship Multiplier M_i(a_d)

The multiplier captures the lost output of students the deceased would have trained.

Step 1: Causal effect per student.
Waldinger (2010, 2012) estimates that dismissal of a chemistry professor reduced the lifetime publication output of each potential doctoral student by \delta = 1.8 papers. This is taken as the per‑student loss parameter.

Step 2: Expected number of future students.
Using the Mathematics Genealogy Project, a Poisson model with age‑specific supervision rate \lambda(a) would estimate the expected additional PhD students had the individual survived. The expected number is:

\mathbb{E}[\text{students} \mid a_d] = \int_{a_d}^{T_{\max}} \lambda(a) S(a) \, da

A simplified projection uses average supervision rates: for a full professor, mean additional students ≈ 3.2.

Step 3: Multiplier formula.

M_i(a_d) = 1 + \delta \cdot \mathbb{E}[\text{students} \mid a_d]

The baseline range is 1.0 (no mentorship effect) to 1.8 (max expected effect). An upper sensitivity bound of 2.5 is reserved for highly prolific senior supervisors.

C.4 Aggregation and Uncertainty Propagation

Future empirical work would use a Monte Carlo simulation (10,000 draws) with distributions specified in Appendix G. This appendix provides the mathematical architecture, not simulation output.

