Appendix D: Synthetic Control Methodology – Counterfactual Estimation Strategy

This appendix details the proposed synthetic control approach. No donor‑pool weights or country‑specific counterfactuals have been computed; the numbers discussed are illustrative benchmarks from the data inventory.

D.1 Donor Pool and Pre‑Treatment Covariates

For each belligerent j, the planned donor pool D_j consists of neutral/non‑belligerent economies with complete data 1920–1970: Sweden, Switzerland, Spain, Portugal, Ireland, and selected Latin American economies (Argentina, Brazil, Mexico, Chile). Pre‑treatment period: 1920–1938. Covariates would include log GDP per capita (Maddison), average years of schooling (Barro‑Lee), log patents per capita (PATSTAT), log publications per capita (WoS), industrial production index (NBER Macrohistory), and change in patents/publications 1933‑1938 to separate Depression effects.

D.2 Matching Algorithm

The Synth package for R would be used to minimise the root mean squared prediction error (RMSPE) over outcome paths and predictor means. Weights are constrained to be non‑negative and sum to 1. Pre‑war fit would be assessed via RMSPE; a synthetic control is considered adequate only if its RMSPE is lower than that of a simple average of donors.

D.3 Expected Challenges and Fallback

· USSR: Donor pool likely insufficient. The primary strategy is a calibrated Romer‑Jones structural model (Appendix E). The synthetic control would be attempted as a robustness check; divergence between the two would widen the uncertainty interval.
· Poland: Small donor pool; growth in publications pre‑war may be poorly matched. Results would be flagged as Tier II.

D.4 Inference

Post‑war treatment effects would be computed as the gap between actual and synthetic series. Placebo tests (assigning treatment to donor countries) would generate a distribution of gaps for inference. All of this remains a planned procedure.

