Appendix E: Romer‑Jones Model Calibration – Structural Fallback Blueprint

E.1 Model Structure

The knowledge production function follows Romer‑Jones:

\dot{A}_t = \delta H_{A,t}^{\lambda} A_t^{\phi}

where A_t is the knowledge stock (cumulative patents or publications), H_{A,t} is human capital in research, \delta a productivity constant, \lambda \in (0,1] captures duplication, and \phi \leq 1 represents intertemporal spillovers.

E.2 Planned Calibration Strategy for the USSR

· H_{A,t}: Estimated from Barro‑Lee and Soviet census data (1939) on scientists and engineers.
· A_t: Cumulative patent applications by Soviet inventors (PATSTAT) or scientific publications.
· A grid search over \lambda, \phi would identify parameter combinations that replicate the observed growth rate of A over 1928‑1938 within ±15%. Only admissible sets would be carried forward.
· Retrodiction check: The calibrated model must reproduce the pre‑war path backward from 1938 to 1928. Sets that fail are discarded.

E.3 No‑War Counterfactual Projection

Under no‑war conditions, H_{A,t} would grow at the pre‑war demographic trend without combat or famine losses. The model would then project \hat{A}_t^{\text{counterfactual}} for 1939‑1970. The flow of new patents (or publications) would be taken as \dot{A}_t. The range of admissible \lambda, \phi would be propagated into the final uncertainty intervals.

