Appendix H: Placebo Test Protocol – 1918 Influenza Pandemic

The framework includes a validation check using the 1918 influenza pandemic. This appendix outlines the protocol; no placebo study has been executed.

H.1 Rationale

The 1918 pandemic produced a large young‑adult mortality shock but without the institutional destruction, prolonged geographic displacement, or network disruption of WWII. If the framework generates implausibly large losses for 1918, its WWII estimates would be suspect.

H.2 Data and Sample Construction

· Excess mortality by city and age from Influenza Encyclopedia (US) and Barro‑Ursúa‑Weng (2020) internationally.
· US excess deaths: ~675,000; ~56% aged 15‑44 → ~378,000 young adults.
· Educational attainment from 1910/1920 US Census IPUMS: ~2.5% of adults 25+ had some college → roughly 9,450 tertiary‑educated victims.
· Knowledge producers identified using the same operational definition (pre‑1920 publication, patent, or university post).

H.3 Application of FHCO Model

The identical FHCO structure would be applied, with:

· Life tables from HMD for 1918.
· Age‑productivity curves from early 20th‑century data.
· Mentorship multiplier set to 1.0 (no institutional destruction), but a minimal network effect could be tested.
· Counterfactual: simple interrupted time‑series or donor‑pool synthetic control (acknowledged as Tier II given global pandemic overlap).

H.4 Expected Benchmarks

Based on the scale of the shock, the framework would be expected to produce a FIM (patents, 20‑year window) in the range of 0.8–1.5, and a FIM (publications) of 0.5–1.2. If the actual implementation yields numbers within these bounds, the framework passes the placebo test. The numbers are not results; they are benchmarks derived from scaling the mortality shock.

