Appendix I: Ethical Architecture – Value Pluralism in the Framework

This appendix formalises the ethical principles embedded in the theoretical architecture. It does not report results but prescribes how values are structurally respected.

I.1 Non‑Summability Protocol

The following operations are explicitly prohibited in any summary table or conclusion derived from the framework:

· Adding Panel A (Direct Stock Losses) to Panel B (Foregone Flow Losses).
· Adding elite FHCO to aggregate Mincerian human capital loss.
· Subtracting war‑induced creation (the “creation ledger”) from destruction to produce a “net balance.”

I.2 Multi‑Criteria Decision Table (Template)

When presenting findings, a matrix of the following form must be used, displaying each dimension in its own value frame:

Loss Dimension Metric Value Frame
Direct stock destruction DSL (1938 $) Replacement cost
Elite innovation foregone FHCO (patents, publications) Meritocratic/elite
Aggregate human capital Mincerian present value Egalitarian
Network fragmentation NDI profile Structural/systemic
Breakthrough loss Nobel‑calibre individuals Extreme tail
Foregone innovation multiple FIM trajectories Macro innovation scale
Moral/cultural loss Qualitative Panel C Irreducible

I.3 Reflexivity Statement (Summary)

The framework’s choice to measure formal knowledge‑system output emerges from a Western, quantitative intellectual tradition. The inclusion of an egalitarian Mincerian measure, the qualitative panel, and the refusal to net destruction against creation are structural acknowledgements of value pluralism. The framework is offered as one disciplined voice, not a final accounting.

